# DOES NOT RUN on this node. Kept for the record, and because the numbers it
# printed before dying are worth having -- see the bottom of this comment.
#
# Observed 2026-08-17, results/6, vLLM 0.27.1, 8x H200. The engine loads the
# weights and sizes its KV cache, then dies during CUDA graph capture:
#
#   Capturing CUDA graphs (decode, FULL):   0%|          | 0/19 [00:01<?, ?it/s]
#   (Worker_DP0_TP1_EP1) WorkerProc hit an exception.
#     File "vllm/model_executor/kernels/linear/scaled_mm/flashinfer.py", line 311,
#       in _dynamic_flashinfer_deepgemm_blockscale_gemm_impl
#     return torch.ops.vllm.dynamic_flashinfer_deepgemm_blockscale_gemm(
#   RuntimeError: CUDA driver error (/workspace/.deps/deepgemm-src/csrc/apis/
#     ../jit_kernels/impls/runtime_utils.hpp:144): 700
#     (CUDA_ERROR_ILLEGAL_ADDRESS, an illegal memory access was encountered)
#   RuntimeError: Engine core initialization failed.
#
# The TP2/DP4 profile dies the same way and also trips FlashMLA's sparse-FP8 MLA
# decode kernel. Both are DeepSeek-V4 fused kernels reached only at TP < 8; the
# recipe pins TP8/DP1 and that is the layout its kernel stack is built for.
#
# Not a memory problem. It got as far as reporting, per DP rank:
#   weights 108.68 GiB/GPU, KV 17.51 GiB/GPU, pool 350,884 tokens
# which is 701,768 tokens node-wide against TP8/DP1's 380,117 -- 1.8x the KV
# capacity, and the reason this layout was worth trying at all.
#
# Untested workaround: --compilation-config {"mode": 0, "cudagraph_mode": "NONE"}
# skips the capture that the error surfaces in. If that loads, the layout works
# and only graph capture is broken -- but then TP8/DP1 has to be measured the
# same way for the comparison to mean anything.
#
# TP4/DP2 with speculative decoding off -- the control for that layout, the way
# deepseek_v4_tp8dp1 is the control for TP8/DP1.
#
# It inherits the layout from deepseek_v4_tp4dp2_speculative and drops the one
# flag, so the pair differs in speculation and nothing else, and both differ from
# the TP8/DP1 pair in layout and nothing else. That is what makes the four a 2x2:
# whether dspark pays for its verify pass can be asked per layout rather than
# measured once and assumed to carry across.
#
# There is reason to think it does not. Speculation pays when decode is
# bandwidth-bound and the wider verify pass is nearly free, and costs when the
# batch is already compute-bound. DP raises the aggregate batch a node can hold,
# so it pushes toward the regime where speculation stops paying.
#
# `off` is how a profile drops a flag its base sets. Restating the flag with a
# value would override it instead.

PROFILE_BASE=NOT_WORKING_deepseek_v4_tp4dp2_speculative

--speculative-config off
